foreign  sociologist Emile durkheim said that one  of the functions of punishment is it  firms up our sense of group solidarity  so when our group's under threat for  some reason when our values are under  threat if we can create an outgroup that  it's legitimate to punish  we can reaffirm our sense of solidarity  I think I think we saw this a bit in the  pandemic our sense of social solidity  was really in Jeopardy but sort of by  definition just social distancing  um it's not a surprise that Boris  Johnson in the summer of 2020 in his  speech about how we're going to recover  from the pandemic said he's building 10  000 new prison spaces  um because it creates this Us and Them  it's a slightly different experience for  me because uh for me this is kind of  where the X was on that map of  concentric circles when I was growing up  my brother was in prison and so it was  my dad and my uncle  my dad's from Liverpool which I think at  the time was quite a sort of a lot of  poverty quite a sort of chromogenic area  my mum's side of the family from the  East End which has a huge kind of uh  historic adversarial relationship with  the police where you could be sort of  excommunicated for talking to a copper  and so for me I've kind of I have a  different relationship to this sort of  out group it's a complex one it's not  one necessarily of unconditional warmth  and regard  um I think I I Googled ambivalence and I  found this wonderful image  um by ambivalence I don't mean the way  it's sometimes misused to say sort of  detached or I don't care or you know a  kind of shrug but this sense of um just  having this kind of cacophonous  uh attitude in your head these multiple  viewpoints  um  you know I was I was greatly pained by  seeing my brother go to prison it seemed  totally unnecessary the War on Drugs  wasn't going to get him to give up his  heroin addiction  um they couldn't possibly punish him  more than he was punishing himself at  the same time when my dad went away I  did feel the kind of relief I felt like  well actually maybe I'd become the  person I am today because I had that bit  of space and uh I sort of lost this kind  of overbearing violent figure in the  house yet within all of that was this  sense of  it could have been me or it will be me  one day a feeling of inherited guilt  that I'm going to be visited by the same  fate and so I couldn't consider my dad's  punishment without thinking well would I  like to be punished that way what would  it do to me how is it going to  how would it deform me further how's it  going to deform him further  um so it's a it was a really sort of  messy uh State  um what I want to do today is say how  what what sort of vantage point that uh  that experience has given me on a more  like social political level when it  comes to uh prisoners and just just what  I've experienced at work and in my  family what happens when we Exile people  so I told you about my family coming  from the East End uh my Uncle Frank uh  when he was 14 uh we did three months in  a bore stall on remand uh if anyone  doesn't know about borstools they're  these kind of short sharp shock regimes  that they used to have where teenagers  would basically have to do hard labor of  a very futile hard labor such as digging  holes that were eight feet deep and then  filling them back in at the end of the  day  um that was a very brutalizing  experience for him shortly after he got  out he was in trouble with the police  again one night when he was 15 he broke  into a corner shop to steal a crate of  Coca-Cola cans he's not really into  Coca-Cola but he's into the buzz of  stealing from shops at that point the  police show up uh they catch him he's  not a very good thief at this stage and  they they think well Coca-Cola is not  really much of a charge but if we can  say that he stole the cash box or was  trying to steal the cash box didn't get  a good charge here so they sat him in  the back of the police car  two police officers either side of him  and they needed to get his fingerprints  on the cash box so they grabbed his  wrists and tried to prize open his hands  uh he just clenched them as tightly as  he could in defiance and they tried to  peel them open and incriminate him  now I I know lots of stories from the  East end that are a bit like that  um the police and ordinary people who  just uh it's a game and they hate each  other the response for someone like my  uncle coming from a working class family  no opportunities to kind of uh very few  opportunities to kind of escape this  confrontation with the police uh was he  thought well if you want to see a  criminal  I'll show you a criminal  uh and it's something that is so echoed  uh uh in in the likes of Pentonville or  Brixton or belmarsh for me I I hear  people just sort of say this all the  time that uh when the states are telling  you that you're awful that you're  criminal when they're stigmatizing you  like that  your way of taking charge of that  situation sometimes is to try and be the  worst possible thing you can be worse  than what even they think you are  um obviously this has really negative  effects for people's behavior and for  Society on the whole uh if that's one of  the only places they have to go uh  there's a man uh in the book  um uh called DK and he talks about how  when he's in prison when he comes back  to prison he's been in and out for much  of his life he feels this kind of sense  that he can relax  and it's not necessarily that he's  talking about being overly  institutionalized although there may be  some of that what he's saying is  in prison when I interact with people  when I I don't know lend someone a tea  bag or a cigarette or when I just chat  to people on the landing uh I'm just  seen to be doing a nice thing to help  someone out outside  it's like I'm stuck behind a  two-dimensional existence  where um  I can't break free people are always  interpreting me according to my  criminality that that's fundamental and  he's sort of he's flattened much more in  the outside world whereas in prison uh  you know  let hearers without sin cast the first  stone people tend to see beyond your  crime and see sort of who you are a bit  more so obviously the the effects of  exiling people is that they will make  another community  but they will make a community amongst  those who will accept them which is all  sometimes a kind of criminal subculture  so I think the way we do things uh our  lack of uh Affinity familiarization or  casting out of the circle of prisoners  is really harmful  uh it leads to more crime pretty much  uh that then brings us to the question  of well what would it mean to feel some  kind of affinity or connection or human  regard for someone in prison that sounds  a bit weird I don't necessarily want to  hold hands and sing Kumbaya with a  murderer you know it's uh we can't be  naive about this we can't be naive about  the fact that people uh can be very  dangerous in prison and they they may be  sort of quarantined from us for a good  reason  I'm going to say that I think there's  two things  that need to change in order for us to I  think have a bit more space and human  regard for people in prison one is to do  with social and material change which is  really really important and the other is  to do with a change of our attitudes how  we think about stuff which you know  talking to people at a philosophy  Festival I'd be really interested to see  what you say about that in the Q and A's  so on the social and material Level  uh this is a more familiar site in  modern Britain  um when I was growing up I kept hearing  this phrase from the kind of working  class Community I lived in which is  um bed and breakfast bed and breakfast  prisons like bed and breakfast I heard  this deep resentment  for the fact that prisoners were getting  three meals a day  and at the moment we are living in a  country where people can go to work they  can work very long hours they can work  every day of the week and they are  skipping meals  um so it's it's one of the sort of  tragic ironies that the the working  classes are not always but uh very often  kind of punitive  they want tougher sentences they want  prison to be harsher  um  I don't take that face value there may  be some people who do I think that  arises from  the feeling of deprivation that people  have in their own life  we talk about Norway and Scandinavia and  these countries that seem to have really  forward-thinking prisons with kind of  the therapeutic interventions and  reintegration programs  the reason they can flourish  is because people's quality of life in  those countries it is generally good  there are very few people in Norway who  are jealous of someone in prison  and I think if we want to change our  prisons we you know I think this is the  general sort of abolitionist sentiment  that we have to uh improve just social  material conditions across the board uh  the second thing that needs to change is  a little bit more esoteric uh it's to do  I think with our kind of attitudes  um uh in the book there's a chapter  called luck and it's about moral luck uh  and I'll explain what that is now but  um I describe being in a classroom  and uh I tell my students about these  two worlds that exist these two  imaginary worlds one is a just world  where good things happen to good people  and bad things happen to bad people and  everything is as it should be it's  perfectly meritocratic  and the other is a luck world  where everything is random whether you  are the judge or the prisoner whether  you're the king or the peasant  uh it's completely random uh I think the  national sport in the luck world would  be Snakes and Ladders if you win at  snakes and ladders it doesn't really  mean anything if you lose it snakes and  that is it doesn't really mean anything  uh in the just world I think uh  chess would probably be their national  sport I don't know a lot about Chess I'm  going on a limb here but as I understand  it it's a game with no random factors  except for who goes first  so uh it's it's it's it's its design is  much more meritocratic I think what's  interesting and difficult about our  world is it's at the murky Borderlands  of these two worlds  is that Justice plays A Part effort  Merit whether you did something good or  bad or not it it's significant but so is  luck right  so is  your where you're born in terms of the  social economic hierarchy  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai.tv for  more debates and talks from the world's  leading thinkers on today's biggest  ideas

